{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments [{:quotes [{:he "ויקוב", :translit "vayikov", :gloss "and he pronounced/blasphemed"} {:he "ויקוב את השם", :translit "vayikov et ha-shem", :gloss "and he blasphemed the Name"} {:he "ויקוב חור בדלתו", :translit "vayikov chor be-delato", :gloss "and bore a hole in its door"} {:he "אימא דברזיה מברז", :translit "ima de-varazai mi-varaz", :gloss "the mother of his vinegar ferments"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["ויקוב את השם" "ויקוב חור בדלתו"]}], :frame {:text "The Aramaic phrase אימא דברזיה מברז, literally 'the mother of his vinegar ferments,' is an idiom meaning that one bad thing leads to another. The Gemara in Sanhedrin 56a uses this idiom in the context of the blasphemer: it teaches that this person committed two evil acts — both the act of blasphemy and a separate quarrel. The Ba'al HaTurim links this rabbinic statement to the Masoretic observation that the verb ויקוב appears exactly twice in Scripture.", :sources ["Sanhedrin 56a"]}} {:quotes [{:he "ויקלל", :translit "vayekallel", :gloss "and he cursed"} {:he "ויקוב ויקלל", :translit "vayikov vayekallel", :gloss "and he blasphemed and cursed"} {:he "ושמעי הולך בצלע ההר לעומתו הלוך ויקלל", :translit "u-shimei holech be-tzela ha-har le-umato haloch vayekallel", :gloss "and Shimei went along the side of the hill opposite him, going and cursing"} {:he "ויקלל הפלשתי את דוד", :translit "vayekallel ha-pelishti et david", :gloss "and the Philistine cursed David"} {:he "ויקלל את יומו באיוב", :translit "vayekallel et yomo be-iyov", :gloss "and he cursed his day in Job"} {:he "אלהים לא תקלל ונשיא בעמך לא תאור", :translit "elohim lo tekallel ve-nasi be-amcha lo ta'or", :gloss "God you shall not curse, and a prince among your people you shall not revile"} {:he "כאחת הנבלות תדברי", :translit "ke-achat ha-nevalot tedaberi", :gloss "as one of the foolish women you speak"} {:he "ויוסף ה׳ לאיוב למשנה", :translit "vayosef ha-shem le-iyov le-mishneh", :gloss "and the L-rd added to Job a double portion"}], :mechanisms [{:kind :masorah, :count 4, :occurrences ["ויקוב ויקלל" "ושמעי הולך בצלע ההר לעומתו הלוך ויקלל" "ויקלל הפלשתי את דוד" "ויקלל את יומו באיוב"]}], :frame {:text "Exodus 22:27 juxtaposes 'God you shall not curse' with 'a prince among your people you shall not revile,' from which the Sages derive that cursing a prince is legally parallel to cursing God. The Ba'al HaTurim applies this to explain the divergent fates of the four figures linked by the root קלל: Shimei, who cursed King David, was ultimately killed by Solomon (1 Kings 2:46); the Philistine Goliath cursed David and was killed (1 Samuel 17); but Job, when his wife urged him to 'curse God and die' (Job 2:9), rebuked her with the words 'as one of the foolish women you speak' (Job 2:10) and cursed only his own day, not God. Therefore Job was rewarded with a double portion of all he had possessed (Job 42:10).", :sources ["Exodus 22:27" "1 Kings 2:46" "1 Samuel 17" "Job 2:9-10" "Job 42:10"]}}], :arithmetic [{:kind :masorah, :count 2, :occurrences ["ויקוב את השם" "ויקוב חור בדלתו"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "ויקוב את השם", :refs [], :n 0, :lens :plene-insensitive} {:phrase "ויקוב חור בדלתו", :refs ["2 Kings 12:10"], :n 1, :lens :plene-insensitive}]} {:kind :masorah, :count 4, :occurrences ["ויקוב ויקלל" "ושמעי הולך בצלע ההר לעומתו הלוך ויקלל" "ויקלל הפלשתי את דוד" "ויקלל את יומו באיוב"], :by :selah, :located [{:phrase "ויקוב ויקלל", :refs [], :n 0, :lens :plene-insensitive} {:phrase "ושמעי הולך בצלע ההר לעומתו הלוך ויקלל", :refs ["2 Samuel 16:13"], :n 1, :lens :plene-insensitive} {:phrase "ויקלל הפלשתי את דוד", :refs ["1 Samuel 17:43"], :n 1, :lens :exact} {:phrase "ויקלל את יומו באיוב", :refs [], :n 0, :lens :plene-insensitive}]}], :book :leviticus, :voice :baal-haturim, :lang :en, :chapter 24, :discipline "bht-chalkboard-v1", :timestamp "2026-07-12T23:25:31.091812788Z", :model "glm-5.2"}